# config/llama-server-re.env
# Configuration for the llama-server inference backend (re slot -- P40 eGPU).
# This file is read by systemd's EnvironmentFile directive.
# The model manager updates MODEL_PATH when swapping models on this slot.
#
# NOT YET DEPLOYED: the P40 is not installed on this host. This file exists
# so the unit and manager wiring are ready ahead of the hardware --
# config/manager.env deliberately leaves `re` out of SLOTS until then. See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-dual-gpu-three-slot-design.md Phase 4.

# Path to the GGUF model file to load on startup. PARE pins gemma-4-26b for
# this slot (PARE/pare/config.py:29 -- "gemma-4-26b handles tool-calling far
# more reliably for PARE's agentic loop").
MODEL_PATH=/opt/llama/models/gemma-4-26b-a4b-it-q4_k_m.gguf

# Sampling flags for whatever MODEL_PATH points at, read by $SAMPLING_ARGS in
# the unit. They live here rather than in ExecStart because this slot is
# swappable: the manager rewrites MODEL_PATH above on a /swap, and sampling
# tuned for one model is wrong for the next. Change both lines together.
#
# Values below are gemma-4-26b-a4b's own published recommendation, read from
# its GGUF (general.sampling.*): temp 1.0, top-p 0.95, top-k 64. Note top-k
# differs from the main slot's Qwen3.8-27B, which wants 20 -- which is exactly
# why this belongs beside the model rather than in a shared unit.
#
# MUST stay unbraced in ExecStart: systemd word-splits $VAR but passes ${VAR}
# as one argument. Empty expands to no arguments and llama.cpp's own defaults
# apply, which are not this model's.
SAMPLING_ARGS=--temp 1.0 --top-p 0.95 --top-k 64

# CUDA device selector. Mirrors config/llama-server.env's DEVICE: CUDA0 names
# an enumeration position, not a card, and is only unambiguous because
# CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES below restricts this process to exactly one physical
# card. See that file's DEVICE comment and the device-pinning section of
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-23-dual-gpu-three-slot-design.md.
DEVICE=CUDA0

# Restricts this process to the P40 by UUID -- see config/llama-server.env's
# CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES comment for how to obtain it and why an ordinal is
# not sufficient. The P40 is not installed on this host yet, so there is
# nothing to query. Once it is installed and enumerates:
#   nvidia-smi --query-gpu=uuid,pci.bus_id,name --format=csv
# and pick the row named "Tesla P40" (the other row is the V100, which
# belongs to config/llama-server.env, not this file). Set
# CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<that uuid> in the deployed copy of this file at
# /etc/llama/llama-server-re.env. Never commit a real UUID here -- it is
# specific to one physical card in one machine. The placeholder below is
# intentionally invalid so this service fails loudly rather than silently
# landing on the wrong card if deployed unedited.
CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-<uuid-of-the-p40-see-comment-above>

# Number of model layers to offload to GPU. -1 = auto (fill all available
# VRAM). The P40 has 24576 MiB; gemma-4-26b's weights are expected to fit
# with all layers offloaded, but that has not been measured on this card.
N_GPU_LAYERS=-1

# Work around a CUDA-backend leak where CUDA graphs are cached per
# generation and not freed, growing host RAM under sustained load
# (llama.cpp issue #20315). See config/llama-server.env for the same note;
# left enabled here as the conservative choice pending its own remeasurement
# on this slot.
GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1

# Context window size in tokens.
#
# PLACEHOLDER -- to be set from measurement, not from projection, and not
# yet measured because the P40 is not installed. Per spec F5, gemma-4-26b at
# ~20 KiB/token plus ~300 MiB of fixed sliding-window cache is *projected* to
# reach its full 262144 native context in roughly 21.8 GiB on the P40's
# 24GB -- that is a number to confirm, not a value to deploy as-is (see F4:
# never set CTX_SIZE from a projection).
#
# Once the card is installed: start this service with a conservative
# CTX_SIZE (the placeholder below), then read llama-server's own allocation
# log for the `llama_kv_cache` line -- `-lv 5` is required to surface it on
# this llama.cpp build (append it to this unit's ExecStart temporarily, or
# run the binary by hand with the same flags plus -lv 5). Set CTX_SIZE from
# that line the way config/llama-server.env's CTX_SIZE was derived from its
# own llama_kv_cache line; re-derive after any model change rather than
# scaling the number.
CTX_SIZE=4096

# Bind address and port. Always localhost -- the model manager is the
# public-facing endpoint.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=8084
